Here is a simple example of how you can create a Tooltip in NextJS using React Context to close the previously opened Tooltip and so on.

```jsx
// TooltipContext.js
import { createContext, useState } from 'react';

const TooltipContext = createContext();

const TooltipProvider = ({ children }) => {
  const [tooltip, setTooltip] = useState(null);

  const openTooltip = (tooltip) => {
    setTooltip(tooltip);
  };

  const closeTooltip = () => {
    setTooltip(null);
  };

  return (
    <TooltipContext.Provider value={{ openTooltip, closeTooltip, tooltip }}>
      {children}
    </TooltipContext.Provider>
  );
};

export { TooltipProvider, TooltipContext };
```

```jsx
// Tooltip.js
import { useContext } from 'react';
import { TooltipContext } from './TooltipContext';

const Tooltip = ({ children, tooltip }) => {
  const { closeTooltip } = useContext(TooltipContext);

  if (!tooltip) return null;

  return (
    <div className="tooltip">
      <div className="tooltip-content">
        {tooltip}
        <button onClick={closeTooltip}>Close</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Tooltip;
```

```jsx
// Button.js
import { useContext } from 'react';
import { TooltipContext } from './TooltipContext';

const Button = ({ children }) => {
  const { openTooltip } = useContext(TooltipContext);

  const handleMouseOver = () => {
    openTooltip('This is a tooltip');
  };

  return (
    <button onMouseOver={handleMouseOver}>
      {children}
    </button>
  );
};

export default Button;
```

```jsx
// App.js
import { TooltipProvider } from './TooltipContext';
import Button from './Button';
import Tooltip from './Tooltip';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <TooltipProvider>
      <Button>Click me!</Button>
      <Tooltip />
    </TooltipProvider>
  );
};

export default App;
```

In this example, we create a `TooltipContext` using the `createContext` function from React. We then create a `TooltipProvider` component that wraps the entire app and provides the `openTooltip`, `closeTooltip`, and `tooltip` state to its children.

The `Tooltip` component uses the `useContext` hook to access the `closeTooltip` function and the `tooltip` state from the context. It renders a tooltip only when the `tooltip` state is truthy.

The `Button` component uses the `useContext` hook to access the `openTooltip` function from the context. When the button is hovered over, it opens the tooltip.

Finally, in the `App` component, we wrap the entire app with the `TooltipProvider` component and render the `Button` and `Tooltip` components inside it. When the button is hovered over, the tooltip is opened and can be closed by clicking the "Close" button. If the button is hovered over again, the previously opened tooltip is closed and a new one is opened. This way, only one tooltip is open at a time.

